y it agreed in 1994 in the budapest  memorandum uh to support ukraine's  territorial integrity and sovereignty if  ukraine gave up its nuclear weapons  ukraine lived up to its end of the  bargain russia has violated as deeply as  you could  andre the fact that the minsk agreements  are not legally binding how much does  that complicate this all  well i think that it might open various  avenues for the russian leadership  in donbass for instance given the  current escalation uh i do not exclude a  diplomatic  recognition of the breakaway republics  by russia and that would be of course a  game changer that would indeed make the  situation east ukraine somewhat similar  to what happened in georgia back in 2008  and then i think uh the minsk agreements  will collapse i don't think that the  probability is very high but i do not  exclude that because in my view the  state duma would have never advanced  such an idea if they had not received a  not from the kremlin  so right now i think this is uh probably  a more  likely challenge to the minsk agreements  than  an invasion of russia across the border  with ukraine at least that's how i see  the situation today  and for me uh the  most immediate task is to preserve the  minsk agreements to avoid the diplomatic  recognition of the breakaway republics  and to get back to the negotiating table  i don't think that we have other  plausible options to reach any progress  in the escalating the situation  kylo i'm curious to get your perspective  on one thing  most people say that the minsk  agreements have essentially been a  failure from the outset  have there been any successes that have  come from them  well look there was an attempt to uh to  push through parliament some changes to  uh to to ukraine's constitution uh those  failed  but we do have uh one of the  requirements the political requirements  of the minsk agreement was to provide uh  in parliament a special law that  provided more autonomy for  uh those areas that 